faq:
  - q: "When is the best time to ask for a raise?"
    a: "Three moments: (1) Before your annual performance review cycle — not during it. Raise decisions are often made before the review conversation happens. Making your case 4–6 weeks before reviews puts you in the conversation while decisions are being made. (2) After a significant accomplishment — you delivered a major project, landed a key account, or solved a high-stakes problem. Strike while the value is visible. (3) When you have a competing offer — the most powerful lever, but use it only if you would genuinely take the offer. Bluffing with a competing offer is high-risk."
  - q: "How do you ask for a raise without it being awkward?"
    a: "Frame it as a business conversation, not a personal ask. 'I want to discuss my compensation in the context of the work I've been doing and where the market is' is professional and direct. 'I really need more money because...' is personal and weak. You are making a business case, not a plea. Prepare your market data, your accomplishment list, and your specific ask in advance — do not go into the conversation to explore, go in to present."
  - q: "How much of a raise should I ask for?"
    a: "Depends on the gap between your current salary and market rate. If you are at market: 5–10% is a reasonable ask for strong performance. If you are 15–20% below market: ask for the gap — anchor to the market data, not to a percentage. If you have been promoted or taken on meaningfully expanded scope: 15–25% is defensible. Calibrate to market data, not to what feels comfortable."
  - q: "What if my manager says no?"
    a: "Ask what would need to change for the answer to be yes. 'I appreciate you being direct. Can you help me understand what would need to happen — performance, timing, or scope — for this to be something we could revisit?' This turns a no into a roadmap. If the answer is vague or non-committal, you have learned something important about whether the ceiling is structural or temporary."
  - q: "Should I show my competing offer when asking for a raise?"
    a: "Only if you would genuinely take the offer. A competing offer is the most effective lever in raise conversations — it converts theoretical market data into real market validation. But using it as a bluff is high-risk: some managers will call it, and some companies have policies against matching competing offers. Use it only when you are prepared to accept the outcome either way."
